PART 3  the dimension count: why the mechanism starts at n = 3
======================================================================
   the mechanism needs a direction field that is DEGENERATE but
   NOT of constant direction.

     n = 2:  v : C -> C^2,   degeneracy det[v', v] = 0
             <=> rank <= 1 <=> CONSTANT direction.  The two
             conditions coincide, and the map trivialises.
     n = 3:  v : C^2 -> C^3, degeneracy det[v_x, v_y, v] = 0
             still admits non-constant solutions -- the
             developables, e.g. Session 1's twisted cubic.
  [PASS] in the plane, degeneracy and constant-direction are the same condition (shown in Part 2)
  [PASS] in dimension 3 they are not: the twisted-cubic field is degenerate and non-constant

